> Four fitting points make projection loss checkable

Take fitting rows [-3,-1],[-3,1],[3,-1],[3,1]. Their mean is [0,0], and their centered
cross-product matrix is diagonal with entries 36 and 4. With N-1=3, sample variances are
12 and 4/3. Retaining the first axis keeps x and removes y. Query [2,0.5] reconstructs to
[2,0]; it does not move the fitting mean or axes. The omitted coordinate is measurable lost
information. PCA signs do not change this inverse projection.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/visualization_toy_pca.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n                     = dm::nn;
  const auto              type    = n::DType::Float64;
  const auto              fitting = n::tensor({-3., -1., -3., 1., 3., -1., 3., 1.}, {4, 2}, type);
  const n::models::SvdPca pca(fitting, 2);
  const auto              query = n::tensor({2., .5}, {1, 2}, type);
  const auto              score = pca.Transform(query), one = n::slice(score, {0, 0}, {1, 1}),
             reconstruction = pca.Reconstruct(one);
  std::print(
      "Variances: {:.6f}, {:.6f}. Query [{:.1f},{:.1f}] reconstructs to [{:.1f},{:.1f}].\n",
      pca.variances().at({0}),
      pca.variances().at({1}),
      query.at({0, 0}),
      query.at({0, 1}),
      reconstruction.at({0, 0}),
      reconstruction.at({0, 1}));
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.points({-3, -3, 3, 3}, {-1, 1, -1, 1}, "Fitting rows", {37, 99, 235}, 4.)
      .lines({-3.5, 3.5}, {0, 0}, "Fitted axis", {22, 163, 74}, 2.)
      .points({2}, {.5}, "Query", {220, 38, 38}, 5.)
      .lines({2, 2}, {.5, 0}, "Discarded y", {100, 116, 139}, 2.)
      .points({2}, {0}, "PCA inverse", {22, 163, 74}, 5.)
      .x_label("Original coordinate x")
      .y_label("Original coordinate y")
      .y_limits(-1.3, 2.3)
      .title("Frozen fitting PCA: one coordinate is discarded")
      .save_svg("build/visualization_toy_pca.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Variances: 12.000000, 1.333333. Query [2.0,0.5] reconstructs to [2.0,0.0].
\image(figures/visualization_toy_pca.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The query projects onto the fitted horizontal axis. Variances use the sample divisor 3, and the query does not refit either axis.)
\alttext(Four rectangle-corner fitting points, a horizontal PCA axis and a query projected vertically to that axis.)

> Neighbor geometry has its own controls

For fitting coordinates [-1,0],[1,0],[0,2] and query [0,0], the first two distances are tied.
The native retrieval API orders equal distances by the smaller fitting row, returning [0,1].
For reference neighbors [0,1] and candidate neighbors [1,2], membership overlap is 1/2.
Only the first neighbor predicts a label in the one-neighbor diagnostic. Labels are supplied
after retrieval and never change distances. This is a fixed Euclidean probe following the
nearest-neighbor rule of [Cover and Hart (1967)](https://isl.stanford.edu/~cover/papers/transIT/0021cove.pdf).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/visualization_toy_neighbors.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n        = dm::nn;
  const auto fitting = n::tensor({-1., 0., 1., 0., 0., 2.}, {3, 2}), query = n::zeros({1, 2});
  const auto neighbors = n::models::NearestFitRows(fitting, query, 2),
             candidate = n::from_vector(std::vector<int>{1, 2}, {1, 2});
  const auto overlap   = n::models::NeighborOverlap(neighbors, candidate);
  const auto accuracy  = n::models::NeighborLabelAccuracy(
      neighbors, n::from_vector(std::vector<int>{4, 8, 9}), n::from_vector(std::vector<int>{4}));
  std::print(
      "Nearest rows: {}, {}; overlap {:.1f}; posthoc first-neighbor accuracy {:.1f}.\n",
      neighbors.at({0, 0}),
      neighbors.at({0, 1}),
      overlap,
      accuracy);
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.points({-1, 1, 0}, {0, 0, 2}, "Fit rows 0,1,2", {37, 99, 235}, 4.)
      .points({0}, {0}, "Query", {220, 38, 38}, 5.)
      .lines({0, -1}, {0, 0}, "First: row 0", {22, 163, 74}, 2.)
      .lines({0, 1}, {0, 0}, "Second: row 1", {100, 116, 139}, 2.)
      .x_label("Coordinate 0")
      .y_label("Coordinate 1")
      .title("Euclidean ties use the original fitting-row index")
      .save_svg("build/visualization_toy_neighbors.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Nearest rows: 0, 1; overlap 0.5; posthoc first-neighbor accuracy 1.0.
\image(figures/visualization_toy_neighbors.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The two nearest fitting rows are equally distant; their deterministic order is fixed by fitting-row index. No labels enter the geometry.)
\alttext(Three fitting points and a query with equal-length links to fitting rows zero and one.)

> A complete visible prerequisite, then frozen analysis

Native MNIST preparation supplies 1,000 fitting, 200 validation and 200 held-out test images,
balanced across ten digits with disjoint original IDs. Test IDs belong to the official test split.
This complete C++ program shows the original 784–32–784 ReLU/sigmoid architecture, seed 1471,
Adam learning rate 0.003, batch-order seed 1483 and all three epochs / 96 updates. Every epoch
visits all 1,000 fitting rows, including the final batch of eight. Validation reconstruction MSE
selects among all three post-epoch checkpoints; digit labels do not enter this fit.

The program also regenerates the original prerequisite's initial/selected model arrays and
metadata. Diagnostic errors use native Float64 tensor reductions. Their tiny reduction roundoff
is recorded separately from exact parameter/prediction comparisons; training time is measured
for this run and is inherently variable. No external model placement or Python preparation is
needed. The projection application that follows adds zero optimizer updates.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/visualization_prerequisite.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <chrono>
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <fstream>
namespace n = dm::nn;
double Score(const n::Tensor& prediction, const n::Tensor& target) {
  return n::mean(
             n::square(n::cast(prediction, n::DType::Float64) - n::cast(target, n::DType::Float64)))
      .item();
}
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::filesystem::path cache = dm::datasets::data_home();
  dm::datasets::prepare_example_data("mnist-assets", cache.string());
  const auto pools      = n::models::LoadSaliency(cache / "saliency");
  const auto fit        = pools[0].images.reshape({1000, 784}),
             validation = pools[1].images.reshape({200, 784}),
             test       = pools[2].images.reshape({200, 784});
  n::ParameterStore p(1471);
  {
    n::NoGradGuard guard;
    n::models::UndercompleteAutoencoder(p, n::zeros({1, 784}));
  }
  if (p.count() != 50992 || p.size() != 4)
    return 1;
  p.save("build/visualization_initial_encoder.npz");
  n::Tensor initial_fit, initial_test, pixel_mean;
  {
    n::NoGradGuard guard;
    initial_fit  = n::models::UndercompleteAutoencoder(p, fit).reconstruction;
    initial_test = n::models::UndercompleteAutoencoder(p, test).reconstruction;
    pixel_mean   = n::cast(n::mean(n::cast(fit, n::DType::Float64), {0}, true), n::DType::Float32);
  }
  n::OptimizerConfig config;
  config.kind = n::OptimizerKind::Adam;
  n::Optimizer        optimizer(p, .003, config);
  n::BatchOrder       batches(1000, 32, 1483);
  double              best     = std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity();
  int                 selected = 0, updates = 0;
  std::vector<double> epochs, training, valid;
  const auto          started = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
  std::print(
      "| epoch | fitting pre-update pixel MSE | validation post-epoch MSE |\n|---:|---:|---:|\n");
  for (int epoch = 1; epoch <= 3; ++epoch) {
    batches.begin_epoch();
    double      total = 0;
    std::size_t seen  = 0;
    for (;;) {
      const auto rows = batches.next();
      if (rows.empty())
        break;
      const auto image = n::take_rows(fit, rows),
                 out   = n::models::UndercompleteAutoencoder(p, image).reconstruction;
      const auto loss  = n::mse(out, image);
      const auto value = loss.item();
      if (!std::isfinite(value))
        return 1;
      total += value * rows.size();
      seen += rows.size();
      optimizer.minimize(loss);
      ++updates;
    }
    n::NoGradGuard guard;
    const auto     error =
        Score(n::models::UndercompleteAutoencoder(p, validation).reconstruction, validation);
    if (seen != 1000 || !std::isfinite(error))
      return 1;
    if (error < best) {
      best     = error;
      selected = epoch;
      p.save("build/visualization_selected_encoder.npz");
    }
    epochs.push_back(epoch);
    training.push_back(total / seen);
    valid.push_back(error);
    std::print("| {} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} |\n", epoch, total / seen, error);
  }
  const double seconds =
      std::chrono::duration<double>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - started).count();
  if (updates != 96 || selected < 1)
    return 1;
  p.load("build/visualization_selected_encoder.npz");
  n::NoGradGuard guard;
  const auto fit_result = n::models::UndercompleteAutoencoder(p, fit).reconstruction;
  const auto test_result = n::models::UndercompleteAutoencoder(p, test);
  std::vector<std::int64_t> shifted;
  for (int row = 0; row < 200; ++row)
    shifted.push_back((row + 1) % 200);
  const auto shuffled = n::models::UndercompleteDecoder(p, n::take_rows(test_result.code, shifted)),
             zero     = n::models::UndercompleteDecoder(p, n::zeros({200, 32}));
  const auto one = n::models::UndercompleteAutoencoder(p, n::take_rows(test, {0})).reconstruction;
  const auto parity = n::max(n::abs(one - n::take_rows(test_result.reconstruction, {0}))).item();
  if (parity >= 1e-6)
    return 1;
  std::ofstream meta("build/visualization_encoder_metrics.json");
  meta.precision(17);
  meta << "{\"epochs\":3,\"selected_epoch\":" << selected << ",\"updates\":" << updates
       << ",\"fit_initial_mse\":" << Score(initial_fit, fit)
       << ",\"fit_mse\":" << Score(fit_result, fit) << ",\"validation_mse\":" << best
       << ",\"test_mse\":" << Score(test_result.reconstruction, test)
       << ",\"test_initial_mse\":" << Score(initial_test, test)
       << ",\"test_mean_mse\":" << Score(n::broadcast_to(pixel_mean, test.shape()), test)
       << ",\"test_black_mse\":" << Score(n::zeros_like(test), test)
       << ",\"test_shuffled_mse\":" << Score(shuffled, test)
       << ",\"test_zero_code_mse\":" << Score(zero, test)
       << ",\"single_batch_max_error\":" << parity << ",\"training_seconds\":" << seconds << "}\n";
  if (!meta)
    return 1;
  std::print(
      "\nSelected epoch {} after {} Adam updates. Held-out MSE {:.9f}.\n",
      selected,
      updates,
      Score(test_result.reconstruction, test));
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.lines(epochs, training, "Fit pre-update", {37, 99, 235}, 2.)
      .lines(epochs, valid, "Valid post-epoch", {220, 38, 38}, 2.)
      .x_label("Epoch")
      .y_label("Mean squared pixel error")
      .title("Visible prerequisite: 96 Adam updates, then a frozen checkpoint")
      .save_svg("build/visualization_prerequisite.svg");
}
```
